<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/6th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Ad Decimum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/533">533</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandalic-war">Vandalic War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Ad Decimum was fought on 13 September 533 at the tenth milestone south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, where the Byzantine general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> defeated the Vandal army of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelimer">Gelimer</a>. Gelimer's attempt to trap the invaders failed, and Belisarius entered Carthage two days later. It opened Justinian's reconquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> from the Vandal Kingdom.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Vandal Kingdom under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilderic">Hilderic</a> kept close ties with Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a>. Hilderic ceased the persecutions and allowed a new Chalcedonian bishop to take office at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, rejecting the Arian Christianity that most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> followed, to the alarm of the Vandal nobility. In 531 his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelimer">Gelimer</a>, a military commander who had campaigned against the Moors, overthrew him. Justinian refused Gelimer's gifts and rejected his legitimacy. Revolts in Tripolitania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, both of which appealed to Justinian, weakened the kingdom further, and Gelimer sent his brother Tzazon with 5,000 Vandals on 150 ships to Sardinia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In June 533 Justinian sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> with 5,000 cavalry, 10,000 infantry, 600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> and 400 Heruls, carried on 500 transports escorted by ninety-two dromons. The army landed at Caputvada and marched toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. On 13 September Gelimer planned an ambush at the narrows of Ad Decimum. His brother Ammatas was to block the path, his nephew Gibamundus was sent with 2,000 men to reinforce him, and Gelimer would attack the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> from the rear.</p><p>The plan unravelled. Ammatas arrived about half a day early with a few men, met the scouts of John the Armenian and was killed after killing twelve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, and his death set off a Vandal rout. In a second encounter Hun mercenaries routed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> at the Salt Marsh. Gelimer later drove back the Byzantine foederati cavalry, but found the body of Ammatas and stopped to mourn instead of pressing his advantage. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> rallied his cavalry and counter-attacked, found the Vandals in disorder and pursued them until nightfall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The army reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> the next evening, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> camped outside, fearing an ambush and wishing to prevent looting. The following day he entered the city in battle formation, found no resistance and occupied the palace. His soldiers kept discipline, though sailors under Calonymus looted merchants' property. He repaired the neglected walls and restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> control of churches held by Arian clergy. The loss of Carthage deprived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> of their main harbour and most of their fleet. In December 533 Belisarius defeated them again at Tricamarum, which ended the Vandal Kingdom.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Ad Decimum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 533 Belisarius beat the Vandals at the <b>Battle of Ad Decimum</b> and the Battle of Tricamarum.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
